{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-quantum-temperature","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:quantum-temperature","kind":"definition","title":"Quantum temperature","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-quantum-entropy","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-planck-time"],"tags":["temperature","statistical-mechanics","reeb-flow","planck-time","non-abelian-stokes","surface-integral"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.Information.quantumTemperature","text":"quantum-temperature.md","leanSource":"quantum-temperature.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:quantum-temperature\"></a><p>Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(\\mathbf{C}, A, \\tau)</code> be a quantum observable universe with substrate parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> and Planck time <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">t_P(q) = q/(q-1) = 1/\\hbar_q</code>. The <strong>quantum temperature</strong> of a region <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U \\subset M</code> is</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  T_q(U) = \\hbar_q = \\frac{q - 1}{q},</code></pre>\n<p>and the <strong>inverse temperature</strong> is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\beta_q = 1/T_q = t_P(q)</code>.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/quantum-temperature.md","heading":"Definition","number":"22.9.3","page":"b/def-quantum-temperature","qa":{"counts":{"pass":82,"fail":0,"warn":1,"na":11,"unknown":0,"stale":68},"milnor":{"total":3,"notPassing":0}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"qou:QOU.Information.quantumTemperature","via":"sibling","path":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/quantum-temperature.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
